 #edb/ref 1034 1.1558263047756883 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050473 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4c6d2580-78c9-5782-9c3a-465dcd055b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1031 "The Distance Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1033 459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050975 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7697398d-61ca-52dc-8fab-4d569b573455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073434 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073434 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $x=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073434 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/95/952be9b27e5350f755f0f534883f3c70516bb4e03b34e9448ab5d43950d4bad6.png)\n\nSince the line $x = 5$ is vertical, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(3,2)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd = |3 - 5| = 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073435 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073435 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073435 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/5c/5ccfa3c27c6bb2759ffc37c73b3640583fa962ee79f47b21f02ac0c1d9164b00.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 5$ and passes through the point $(1,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-2x+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{2}x-5 \\\\ y &=-2x+5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by setting the two equations equal and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =-2x+5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+2x & =5+5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $\\left( 4,-3\\right).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,3)$ and $\\left( 4, -3 \\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-1)}^{2}+{(-3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is $3 \\sqrt{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073436 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073436 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c48fc163b3c9543dcfdd03b20882f0d2a19747672c6b1db2a42a683f0014fe25.png)\n\n \n\nFind the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073436 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x & =4 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y =  x + 4$ and passes through the point $(3,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=x+4 \\\\ y &=-x+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =-x+6 \\\\ x+x & =6-4 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is  $(1,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186488349 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073810 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484379 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484383 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186484390 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186484391 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073811 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186342212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488386 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488392 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186488393 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073815 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1045 "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186073946 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f6ae1858-5ee1-5d31-814d-c2d09d20e0b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074513 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074513 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074513 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074513 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f97972b1765e58a76475a99a97e4a8818b05efa1f26a79a232b250b6ef681e9b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074513 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2a5b92a-6d2c-561b-999b-9dedaf72311f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074513 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186074514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074514 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d4/d478fb4499ee19a7c385ae93dd5ad790404351ceda63c20341b9996a1466ca60.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "853cfa3c-da08-562e-b7e9-24b69cddbfcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074514 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186074759 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074759 #edb/ref 1061 45229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074759 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be614ac7-a2e7-5e60-8bca-ad9de9ca9731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074759 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074760 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074760 #edb/ref 1061 45230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074760 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f8070e4-2aac-5a71-be57-98f43a5de8dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074761 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074761 #edb/ref 1061 45233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074761 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4482ee1-af85-5a92-b2a1-929166abc8b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074761 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074762 #edb/ref 1061 45234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37db8424-1ef2-55d0-b76f-cd55f5ac5469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074767 #edb/ref 1061 45522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14381924-124a-5e92-85f9-6c4bc617cf97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074768 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074768 #edb/ref 1061 45600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a2673e24-58fd-5d2a-98b3-03e91da98a5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075026 #edb/ref 1064 "The shortest distance between a point and a line is the length of the line segment that connects the point to the line and is perpendicular to the line.\n\nFor example, consider the point ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) and the horizontal line ![](images/06/06444505f504ecd01a91d4846b46bf202caafa9d9f756958483262f69b5db14b.svg) shown below:\n\n![](images/8c/8cba6df956e809981ac4a3ae78a6f6843be0ca8561fbcadd9d9ba98f2a5325cc.png)\n\nTo find the distance between the point and the line, we construct a segment with its endpoints on the point and the line, and which is perpendicular to the line\n\n![](images/80/802b30a788850457206716430755da2d5e3c6b3a53633c95626e49ee668d2507.png)\n\nThe distance between the point and the line is the length of this segment, which we compute as the absolute value of the difference in ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the point and the line:\n\n![](images/11/11e3682c2b811da299d9c475bd0ba3684d0c7f68e5677d925744b37026c47b0c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075026 #edb/ref 1065 7819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075026 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1d8ad60c-5b38-530d-abc1-d03020dca8b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075026 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075027 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the line $l$ with the equation $y=-x + 6$ and the point $Q(2,1)$ that does not lie on $l.$\n\n![](images/83/8353825890413432e74d30512219cb95edc76fe8c24e7a824d6893c745c48bd1.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find the point $P$ that lies on $l$ that's closest to the point $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/f8/f880cfde6bdfe07c6804609f9b052189632afcde4907207de5245298aea9ea6e.png)\n\nNow, here's the key idea: $P$ is the point that lies on $l$ *and* lies on the line *perpendicular* to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\nSo, to find $P,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the line perpendicular to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\n- Then, we find the intersection of the two lines.\n\nWe know that one endpoint of the segment is [math](2,1).[/math] To find the other endpoint, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to [math]y=-x+6[/math] and passes through the point [math](2,1),[/math] and then find where it intersects [math]y=-x+6.[/math]\n\nThe line $y=-x + 6$ has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1$ (the negative reciprocal). It also must pass through the point $(2,1).$ So, using point-slope form, we find that the equation of the perpendicular line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =x-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the lines $y=-x+6$ and $y=x-1$ intersect, we set the equations equal to each other and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =x-1 \\\\ 6+1 & =x+x \\\\ 7 & =2x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ So, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( \\dfrac{7}{2}, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075027 #edb/ref 1065 7820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075027 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8fd7f6d4-4f53-50f8-9d72-cceb3d3315ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075027 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Point on a Line Closest to Another Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075028 #edb/ref 1064 "Earlier, we found that the point $P\\left(\\dfrac72,\\dfrac52\\right)$ lies on the line $y=-x+6$ and is the closest point on this line to the point $Q(2,1).$\n\n![](images/11/11901390427d5cd79af514a589175044cafebf47becdae84045005f4564de090.png)\n\nTo calculate the shortest *distance* between $Q$ and the line, we compute the distance between $P$ and $Q$ using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2}-2)}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2}-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shortest distance between the line $y=-x+6$ and $Q(2,1)$ is $\\dfrac{ 3 }{ \\sqrt{2} } .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075028 #edb/ref 1065 7916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075028 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb4d84ad-bfc1-5e9f-9da6-1447abdc11bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075028 #edb/ref 1067 "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339162 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,3)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339162 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339162 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0498568d-b327-5f78-89e8-8587c0368b6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339938 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339938 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339938 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59078a96-a4c1-5355-9bdf-3b0f39c93291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340009 #edb/ref 1080 "(0,-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340009 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340009 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186340259 #edb/ref 1080 "(-\\frac{9}{2},