7592186045436] [17592186429495 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050190 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429496 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429496 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 526->408. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429496 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049869 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429497 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429497 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 526->742. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429497 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049060 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186484379 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484389 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484379 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484379 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,15)$ and the line $y=x -6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99d5220d-0d33-5926-9ea0-c7324f7866f5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484379 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/c2/c2a38b553533a53e06a32db62890e7c91cf5c4f827a14a9fe9028dc18c399690.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x -6$ and passes through the point $(-3,15).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(15) & =-1(x-(-3)) \\\\ y & =12 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x -6 \\\\ y=12 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x -6 & =12 -x \\\\ (2)x & =18 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=9$ into either of the two equations gives $y=3.$ So, our solution is $x=9$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(9,3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,15)$ and $(9,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{(9-(-3))^2+(3-(15))^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{144+144} \\\\ & =12 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,15)$ and the line $y=x -6$ is $12 \\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484383 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484381 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484383 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484383 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484383 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c182161a-7f52-5240-b03d-e600d3d24498" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484383 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x + 2 y & =-42 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/bb/bbd49aa51f1721cb274e2fe4451aaec3319884eff891165fe0a9e941b04b4a70.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21$ and passes through the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-(14)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 \\\\ y=\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6} \\\\ (-\\frac{13}{6})x & =\\frac{91}{6} \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-7$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-7$ and $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and $(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{(-7-(14))^2+(-\\frac{21}{2}-(\\frac{7}{2}))^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{441+196} \\\\ & =7 \\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42$ is $7 \\sqrt{13}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484380 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484390 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-15,9)$ and the line $5 x + 2 y=30.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9e40c34-20e6-50f1-adaa-a0e86b153a34" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186484390 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 x + 2 y & =30 \\\\ y & =15 -\\frac{5 x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/ee/ee6ae236d06003f7c77eb6bf3f9e6c4ce1c317a9e08c9facf04b0a7f8065f9e2.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=15 -\\frac{5 x}{2}$ and passes through the point $(-15,9).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(9) & =\\frac{2}{5}(x-(-15)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2 x}{5} + 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=15 -\\frac{5 x}{2} \\\\ y=\\frac{2 x}{5} + 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 -\\frac{5 x}{2} & =\\frac{2 x}{5} + 15 \\\\ (-